# Spring Boot Podman and Quadlet Contract

These rules govern container images and Podman deployments for Spring Boot
projects. Use Podman and Quadlet with systemd for persistent container services.
Do not introduce Docker Engine or Docker Compose in new deployments. Treat existing
Docker/Compose files as legacy input when the user explicitly asks to work on them;
do not migrate an existing project solely to enforce this default. Service-specific
contracts own the configuration and behavior of databases, brokers, caches, secret
stores, and proxies.

## Ownership

- Defer application YAML, profiles, and secrets to `spring-boot-config.mdc`.
- Defer HTTPS and certificate handling to `spring-boot-tls.mdc`.
- Defer certificate and secret-file ignore patterns to `spring-boot-gitignore.mdc`.
- Defer health endpoint exposure and authorization to `spring-boot-actuator.mdc`
  and `spring-boot-security.mdc`.
- Defer human-facing container commands and prerequisites to
  `spring-boot-readme.mdc`.
- Keep service-specific infrastructure settings in the contract for that service.

## Image builds

- Use Podman to build and run container images.
- Name new image build files `Containerfile` and new ignore files `.containerignore`.
- Use a multi-stage build for deployable application images.
- Keep compilation and dependency resolution in the builder stage; keep the runtime
  stage limited to the application artifact and runtime requirements.
- Copy a locally built JAR into a runtime image only as an explicitly development-only
  workflow.
- Use a base image whose Java runtime matches the project's configured Java version.
- Prefer maintained minimal runtime images. Custom base images are optional, but their
  Java version, user, filesystem layout, and update process must be documented.
- Pin image references to explicit versions. Use immutable digests for production
  deployments when the deployment platform supports them.
- Keep build context small with a focused `.containerignore`.
- Do not copy source-controlled secrets, local environment files, build caches, or
  unrelated workspace files into an image.

## Runtime identity and hardening

- Run application processes as a non-root user inside the container.
- Declare or verify the image's runtime user explicitly; do not rely on undocumented
  base-image behavior.
- Select rootless user Quadlet or rootful system Quadlet deliberately and document the
  reason. Do not select system units only for convenience.
- Use Quadlet `[Container]` settings such as `DropCapability=ALL` and
  `NoNewPrivileges=true` unless a documented runtime requirement needs an exception.
- Use `ReadOnly=true` with the supported temporary mounts when the process allows it.
- Mount certificates and configuration read-only; make only required persistent data
  paths writable.
- Do not grant privileged mode, host networking, or extra capabilities without an
  explicit operational requirement.

## Quadlet and systemd lifecycle

- Define persistent containers with Quadlet source files rather than handwritten
  generated unit files or detached container startup commands.
- Pin fully qualified image references in each Quadlet definition.
- Put boot activation in the Quadlet `[Install]` section using the correct target for
  the selected user or system unit scope.
- Do not run `systemctl enable` on generated Quadlet service units.
- Use the matching `systemctl` scope for reload, startup, status, logs, restart, and
  shutdown operations.
- For rootless user services that must start without an interactive login, configure
  and document user lingering.
- Validate Quadlet syntax with the generator shipped with the target Podman version
  before starting the service.
- Verify reboot startup separately from unit syntax and initial startup checks.

## Profiles, resources, and configuration

- Select Spring profiles explicitly through the Quadlet environment configuration.
- Use `production` for production deployments and select `development` explicitly for
  local development; follow the Spring Boot configuration contract for profile files.
- Set CPU and memory limits for production-like services using supported Quadlet
  resource options.
- Keep credentials out of tracked Quadlet units and environment files. Use Podman
  secrets or a separately protected environment file from an approved secret source.
- Keep `.env.example` free of secrets and label local-only values clearly.
- Use required environment substitutions for values that must be supplied before
  service startup.

## Health, networking, and persistence

- Add a container health check when the image or application has a reliable check.
- Use the application's Actuator liveness or readiness endpoint when Actuator is
  enabled; align its scheme and port with the TLS termination boundary.
- Use Podman networks for containers communicating on the same host. For services on
  different VMs, use stable host addresses and explicit firewall rules; container DNS
  names do not span VM boundaries by default.
- Give persistent data explicit host paths or Podman volumes with documented ownership
  and runtime UID/GID mapping.
- Do not treat container replacement as a data backup strategy.
- Publish host ports only when an explicit client or deployment boundary requires them.

## Logging and TLS boundaries

- Write application container logs to stdout/stderr so Podman and the selected host log
  driver can collect them.
- Keep rolling file logging only when the deployment explicitly requires local files;
  use a documented writable mount for those files.
- Do not assume a file-only application logger is visible through container logs.
- Default application containers to HTTP when a trusted reverse proxy terminates TLS.
  Use embedded HTTPS only when the TLS contract and deployment boundary require it.
- Document whether TLS terminates in the application, a proxy, or another ingress layer.

## Local-only services

- Mark unauthenticated services, insecure dashboards, broadly published infrastructure
  ports, disabled transport encryption, and development certificates as local-only.
- Do not carry local-only insecure defaults into production deployments.
- Keep service credentials in Podman secrets or an approved protected secret source.
- Use explicit health checks and persistence settings for local infrastructure services,
  but defer service-specific authentication and topology rules to their own contracts.

## Forbidden

- Never introduce Docker Engine or Docker Compose for a new deployment.
- Never build a production image from a runtime that uses a different Java major
  version than the application requires.
- Never run a deployable application process as root without a documented exception.
- Never use privileged containers, host namespaces, or broad capabilities as a
  convenience workaround.
- Never commit environment files containing secrets, private keys, keystores, or real
  credentials.
- Never publish databases, brokers, caches, secret stores, or administration dashboards
  beyond their explicit trust boundary.
- Never claim a service is ready based only on a running container; use a meaningful
  health check and application-level validation.
- Never assume stdout and rolling-file logging are equivalent without documenting the
  chosen collection path.
